        <p class="profile-detail-region">Great work · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Hagia Sophia</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">532 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Built for the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> as the Christian basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; designed by the geometers Isidore of Miletus and Anthemius of Tralles; more than ten thousand people employed; the dome rebuilt by Isidore the Younger after 558; buttresses and minarets added under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> by the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A domed basilica of brick and mortar on a vast oblong plan, its central dome on four pendentives rising 55.6 m above the floor on an arcade of 40 windows, extended by two half domes; marble revetment and floors, mosaics, and four Ottoman minarets</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Standing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, where it is the Hagia Sophia Grand Mosque, with parts open to visitors outside prayer times; the Fossati brothers’ drawings of its mosaics are kept in the Archive of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Canton of Ticino</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">its pendentive dome and the light that makes it appear to hover; the world’s largest church for more than five hundred years; its mosaics, among them the Deësis; the coronations and ceremonies of the Byzantine emperors; its conversions from cathedral to mosque, museum and mosque again; its influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches and Ottoman mosques</span></div>
        </div>
