    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Completed in 537 as the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, it became the world’s largest interior space and among the first buildings to employ a fully pendentive dome.</li><li>It served as a cathedral until 1453, except for 1204–1261, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> installed their own hierarchy; then as a mosque, from 1935 as a museum, and since 2020 as a mosque again.</li><li>It became the model for Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church architecture, and its style was emulated by Ottoman mosques a thousand years later.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Runic graffiti in the northern gallery are believed to have been left by members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>, and a 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> inscription in the southern section reads “Halvdan was here”.</p></aside>
    </section>

